\"And I did not have another thobe at the time.\" Now, keep in mind, this is Ka'b ibn Malik, who is one of the biggest poets in Medina, and actually the whole Arabian Peninsula. But that just shows you that the people from Medina had it really rough. Another one that also suggested that times were really tough in Medina. Is a narration of Abu Ayyub al-Ansari which we find in yes again in Bukhari and Muslim. In which he says that the prophet peace be upon him said that if you're going to basically defecate, then do not face the qibla and do not have it behind you like Yes, you know, be towards the side or at a diagonal or something.", "In any case, yeah, Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, remember he's from Banu Najjar and like keep these things in mind like the names of these clans they're beneficial makes you enjoy these reports more. He says and then when we went to Sham we found the marahiid that were built facing the Kaaba. Okay, the marahiid of course any Arab today will tell you that a marhaab is like a toilet. And the report doesn't mention anything much more other than that but he says yeah, like we used to basically use the toilets while leaning towards a different direction. What's really interesting here is that Abu Ayyub al-Ansari is speaking of this in a way that indicates that they didn't have toilets in Medina.", "Right, I mean we could have guessed that but what's what's interesting here is that he's saying that they did have them in a Sham. In a Sham, these were built in toilets. Um and in Sham at least parts of it perhaps towards the north, they did seem to have some sort of drainage system. Um and like even if you look here on the map, here you have Bosra in a Sham which is of course under Byzantine control. You can see like it's a walled city, it's a really good looking city compared to like you know the run down archaic structures that you find in the Hijaz and Um, good chunk of the Arabian Peninsula.", "You actually do see the Byzantine infrastructure um, affecting the people of Bosra or Bosra itself. So, what do we benefit from this? I mean, a lot of things. If you connect these dots together um, and you look at all these matters holistically, it seems like, you know, these people that uh, even the poets themselves don't have more than one thobe. You have the leader not having um, a horse, but you know, only having a donkey. The people not having proper toilets. Despite all of that, they were able to succeed in spreading the message and spreading their influence over the whole region.", "And not only that, but conquer their enemies, defeat their foes from the Byzantines and the Sassanids, which were the two strongest empires at the time. Subhanallah.",))
